# What never enters the build context.
#
# Two reasons, and the second one is the important one:
#   1. Size and speed -- a smaller context is a faster build and a smaller image.
#   2. Safety -- an uploaded extract, a run folder or a trained model must not be
#      copied into an image, because an image gets shared and a bind mount does
#      not. Everything the services read at runtime is mounted, never baked in.

# --- virtualenvs: huge, and a Windows .venv is useless inside a Linux image
.venv/
.venv_*/
venv/
env/

# --- Python noise
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.pytest_cache/
.ipynb_checkpoints/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/

# --- version control and editor state
.git/
.github/
.gitignore
.gitattributes
.vscode/
.idea/

# --- data: mounted read-only at runtime, never carried in an image
data/

# --- uploaded files and their analysis runs: these hold data someone handed us
runs/
.run/
logs/
*.log

# --- artifacts: mounted at runtime (read-only for the services, writable for the
#     trainer profile), so the image must not contain a stale copy
artifacts/

# --- secrets and local environment. .env.example is documentation, not a secret,
#     but it is not needed in an image either.
.env
.env.*

# --- development-only
tests/
notebooks/
docs/
scripts/
Makefile
docker-compose*.yml
Dockerfile*
.dockerignore

# --- documentation: README is the one a reader of an image might want, and it is
#     small, so it is the single exception
*.md
!README.md
